.TH "WEBTRANSPORT.DRAINING" "3JS" "September 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
WebTransport.draining \- WebTransport: draining property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]draining\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]WebTransport\f[R] interface returns a \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that
fulfills when the server indicates that it would like the transport
session to start draining, prior to closing it.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]Promise\f[R] that resolves to \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
A session enters the \f[I]draining\f[R] state when signaled by the
server.
This signal might be sent if the session (and potentially the underlying
connection) is going to be torn down soon, for example because the
server is rebalancing load across backend instances, enforcing a maximum
session lifetime, or even restarting.
Draining is only a request: either endpoint may continue using the
session, and may still open new streams or send datagrams, but the
expectation is that the application will start wrapping up and close the
session soon.
.PP
A web application can use the \f[CR]draining\f[R] promise to
preemptively and gracefully handle the expected shutdown.
It might, for example, finish the current task at a natural stopping
point, and open a new session to continue the work.
.PP
The returned promise is created when the \f[CR]WebTransport\f[R] object
is created and is fulfilled when the session enters the draining state.
Accessing or awaiting this promise does not trigger any action; it only
allows actions to be taken when the session starts draining, similar to
listening to an event.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Migrating to a new transport on draining
In this example we first define an \f[CR]initTransport()\f[R] function
that creates a connection and waits for it to be usable:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] url = \(dqhttps://example.com:4999/wt\(dq;

\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] initTransport(url) {
  \f[I]// Initialize transport connection\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] transport = \f[B]new\f[R] WebTransport(url);

  \f[I]// The connection can be used once ready fulfills\f[R]
  \f[B]await\f[R] transport.ready;

  \f[B]return\f[R] transport;
}

\f[B]let\f[R] transport = \f[B]await\f[R] initTransport(url);
.EE
.PP
We then define a \f[CR]migrateOnDraining()\f[R] function that waits for
the server to indicate that the session should start draining.
This creates a new transport and returns it, and also closes the old
transport.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] migrateOnDraining(url, oldTransport) {
  \f[I]// Promise fulfills when signaled by server\f[R]
  \f[B]await\f[R] oldTransport.draining;

  \f[I]// Open a replacement session so the ongoing feed isn\(aqt interrupted\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] newTransport = \f[B]await\f[R] initTransport(url);

  \f[I]// Switch to sending further data on newTransport instead of oldTransport\f[R]
  \f[I]// \&...\f[R]

  \f[I]// The old session is no longer needed once the new one is in use\f[R]
  oldTransport.close();

  \f[B]return\f[R] newTransport;
}

\f[I]// Not awaited: settles when draining starts\f[R]
migrateOnDraining(url, transport).then((newTransport) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  transport = newTransport;
});
.EE
.PP
Note that while we close the session above when draining is signaled, we
would still need to handle the case when the session is closed by some
other mechanism.
This code waits on the promise returned by
\f[CR]WebTransport.closed\f[R] and logs whether the session closed
gracefully or unexpectedly.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]async\f[R] \f[B]function\f[R] watchForClose(transport) {
  \f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] { closeCode, reason } = \f[B]await\f[R] transport.closed;
    console.log(\(gaSession closed gracefully (code ${closeCode}): ${reason}\(ga);
  } \f[B]catch\f[R] (error) {
    \f[I]// The session can close abruptly, without draining being signaled first\f[R]
    console.error(\(gaSession closed unexpectedly: ${error}\(ga);
  }
}

watchForClose(transport);
.EE
